{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Who.</b> מי applies to a person, not to an animal, and it is a noun. The meaning: who placed you, or who are you — or who is your name, by way of brevity — and likewise here: who caused this transgression of Jacob. And Rabbi Marinus said: for מי את בתי is defective, as מי were you; and here מי פשע — who did it, and who was the root of Jacob's transgression — and it amounts to the same thing." "<b>Is it not Samaria.</b> The meaning: the inhabitants of Samaria, and likewise Jerusalem [i.e., its inhabitants]."], :book :micah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The phrase מי את בתי (lit. \"who are you, my daughter\") appears to be a cited example from another context, possibly 2 Samuel 14; the notation חסר (defective, i.e., without vav) is applied to בתי."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Micah -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=20&perek=1&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:13:34.404997834Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}